Subject: Re: Is the console output considered a public interface?
Content-Type: multipart/alternative;
 boundary=e34b9aa463b243e8b91ee3c69ffaaa63
From: "Kirk True" <kirk@kirktrue.pro>
Reply-To: <dev@kafka.apache.org>

--e34b9aa463b243e8b91ee3c69ffaaa63
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable

Hi all,

It would be good to come right and say that the output is not considered=
 an "interface" if that's what's decided. Some of the command line tools=
 produce structured output that suggest they could be relied on to scrap=
e for various purposes.

Also, there are some system tests that rely on the output of command lin=
e tools, so we should encourage developers to run relevant system tests =
when the output format is changed. (Either that or change the tests.)

Thanks,
Kirk

On Mon, Dec 1, 2025, at 1:40 PM, Matthias J. Sax wrote:
> I also agree, that the output is not part of the public interface, for=20
> all the reason stated.
>=20
> When we refer to monitoring, my read is that it's about JMX metrics,=20
> which we cannot just add/remove or change.
>=20
> And when we refer to command lines tools, it's about the existence of =
a=20
> tool (we cannot just remove it), and it's input (ie, command line=20
> parameters), to not break any scripts / automation which is=20
> using/invoking the cli tools.
>=20
>=20
> -Matthias
>=20
> On 11/30/25 10:02 PM, jian fu wrote:
> > Hi:
> >=20
> > I feel that it makes more sense for the output not to be considered a
> > public interface. If we treat it as one, it implicitly suggests that=
 the
> > output is a very stable dependency. Users might even build logic aro=
und
> > it=E2=80=94for example, counting how many lines contain certain keyw=
ords=E2=80=94which
> > would further restrict what we can do in the future. In reality, it=E2=
=80=99s just
> > output, and it=E2=80=99s something that can even be controlled by lo=
g4j to
> > determine whether it is displayed at all.
> >=20
> > Regards
> > Jian
> >=20
> > Andrew Schofield <andrew_schofield@live.com> =E4=BA=8E2025=E5=B9=B41=
2=E6=9C=881=E6=97=A5=E5=91=A8=E4=B8=80 07:06=E5=86=99=E9=81=93=EF=BC=9A
> >=20
> >> Hi Chia-Ping,
> >> I=E2=80=99m going to jump in and get the discussion started.
> >>
> >> My view is that the output of the console tools is NOT a formal pub=
lic
> >> interface. If it was, the KIPs would have to be totally explicit in=
 the
> >> specification of the output formats, down to the spacing, column wi=
dths in
> >> tables, blank lines and so on. We would also need a major release t=
o make
> >> even minor formatting changes unless they were protected by an opti=
on.
> >>
> >> I think the console tools are wrappers around the admin API, and th=
at=E2=80=99s
> >> the public interface. If someone wants to build automation around K=
afka, it
> >> would be much better to use the admin API rather than the console t=
ools.
> >> For interfaces which are intended to have a human using them direct=
ly, I
> >> think we should be prepared to improve them in ways that a human wi=
ll
> >> instantly understand but which might confuse a piece of unsophistic=
ated
> >> software capturing and parsing the output.
> >>
> >> In a similar vein, would the log lines produced by a broker be cons=
idered
> >> a public interface? People will certainly be scraping those to unde=
rstand
> >> broker information. I=E2=80=99d say that the log lines should also =
not be
> >> considered a formal public interface. I don=E2=80=99t think we want=
 people relying
> >> on specific strings in the log lines, or the order in which they are
> >> emitted.
> >>
> >> Just my 2 cents,
> >> Andrew
> >>
> >>
> >> On 29 Nov 2025, at 20:02, Chia-Ping Tsai <chia7712@gmail.com> wrote:
> >>
> >> hi all,
> >>
> >> I opened KAFKA-19941 to improve the feature tool output, but now we=
 face a
> >> compatibility question: Does our definition of a public interface, =
which
> >> includes "monitoring" and "command line tools" (per the KIP page), =
cover
> >> the output format?
> >>
> >> any feedback?
> >>
> >> Best,
> >> Chia-Ping
> >>
> >>
> >=20
>=20
>=20

--e34b9aa463b243e8b91ee3c69ffaaa63--

