Kamal Chandraprakash resolved KAFKA-19970.
------------------------------------------
    Fix Version/s: 4.3.0
       Resolution: Fixed

> Add time-based eviction to tiered storage index cache to prevent stale en=
tries from accumulating
> -------------------------------------------------------------------------=
-----------------------
>
>                 Key: KAFKA-19970
>                 URL: https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-19970
>             Project: Kafka
>          Issue Type: Bug
>          Components: Tiered-Storage
>            Reporter: Nandini Singhal
>            Assignee: Nandini Singhal
>            Priority: Major
>             Fix For: 4.3.0
>
>
> The remote log index cache (RemoteIndexCache) currently only uses weight-=
based eviction. This can cause old, smaller index files to remain cached in=
definitely while newer indices thrash the cache, leading=C2=A0to inefficien=
t cache utilization and increased remote fetch failures.
> (RemoteIndexCache.java:142-144):
> {code:java}
> =C2=A0 return Caffeine.newBuilder()
> =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 .maximumWeight(maxSize)
> =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 .weigher((Uuid key, Entry entry) -> (i=
nt) entry.entrySizeBytes)
> =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 .evictionListener(...)
> =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 .build();{code}
> =C2=A0 In environments with:
> =C2=A0 - Heavy backfill workloads (reading old data once, then moving to =
newer data)
> =C2=A0 - Sequential read patterns through tiered storage
> =C2=A0 - Variable index file sizes
> =C2=A0 The cache can end up in a state where:
> =C2=A0 - Old index files from completed backfills remain cached=C2=A0
> =C2=A0 - Newer index files thrash continuously
> =C2=A0 - Cache hit rate degrades over time
> =C2=A0 - Increased remote storage fetch errors due to cache misses
> =C2=A0
> Add time-based eviction using Caffeine's expireAfterAccess to the cache c=
onfiguration so that even if an entry remains in a favorable frequency buck=
et, it will be evicted after=C2=A0not being accessed for the configured dur=
ation.



